course: dictionary
lesson: 3046
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around them, tells you which meaning the writer wants. Earwig is a good example: it can name a small creepy-crawly, or it can mean listening in on people.
explain: In «Earwigs don't seek out ears and can't eat brains», the clues are ears, brains and the talk about myths. This is about the insect. Ask yourself what would fit here, then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: In «Stop earwigging!» the -ing ending and the words around it show that someone is listening secretly to other people's talk. Here earwig is a verb, a doing word, and it is informal, so you would hear it in everyday speech rather than in a science book.
fact: An earwig is a small brown insect with a long body and a pair of pincers at its tail end.
fact: Earwigs are minibeasts. You can find them under stones, bark and flowerpots.
fact: Earwigs eat plants and small insects.
fact: Myth: earwigs do not crawl into people's ears to lay eggs. Their name probably comes from old folk beliefs.
fact: In UK informal speech, «earwigging» means listening secretly to other people's conversations.
exercise: «Earwigs don't seek out ears and can't eat brains.» In this sentence, what is an earwig? | A tall tree | A kind of bird | A secret listener | *A small brown insect with a long body
exercise: Lottie's brother whispers at bedtime that earwigs crawl into your ears at night. What is the true fact? | Earwigs crawl into ears to hunt for food | *Earwigs don't seek out ears | Earwigs live in the sea | Earwigs only come out in the day
exercise: «Stop earwigging!» What does earwigging mean here? | Eating plants in a flowerpot | Crawling under a stone | *Listening secretly to other people's conversations | Flying around the garden
test: In «daddy longlegs are harmless to people», what does harmless mean? | Flying fast | Very hungry | *Not dangerous, so they do no harm | Very loud
test: Find the word in «earwigs got their spooky name from old folk beliefs» that means very old. | spooky | name | *old | beliefs
exam: A nosy child is told off for «earwigging». Is the word about an insect or about listening in? | A flowerpot | An insect | A garden pest | *Listening in
